WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.501
-Our first guest
is an Emmy-winning

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:06.238
and Oscar-nominated actress
who stars in the new series

00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:08.174
"Nine Perfect Strangers,"

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.910
which premieres this Wednesday
on Hulu.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:14.847
Please welcome Melissa McCarthy!

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:17.984
Welcome back. Now...

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:20.620
Thank you so much
for being here, bud.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.789
And thanks for playing
that game.

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:25.458
Is it odd to hear
that's the future?

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:29.362
Like, did you just have to do
syllables when you did that?

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:31.497
-I did both.

00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:34.934
It was an immense amount of
recording where you would

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:38.905
just say strange parts of words,
sometimes letters,

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:42.308
sometimes whole paragraphs
that were, like, medical,

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:45.011
and, like, a list of Norwegian
names, and I'm like,

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:46.579
"How often is this
gonna come up?"

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:47.747
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, exactly.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.515
-And it would just be
the most bizarre --

00:00:49.515 --> 00:00:51.718
like, it was, like,
a little bit of everything.

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:53.286
-Did those trip you out?
-And you had to say it

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:56.456
without stopping, so we would do
like 1,500 chunks

00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:58.257
of dialogue a day.

00:00:58.257 --> 00:01:01.961
It was fun and,
like, completely different.

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:03.396
I've never done
anything like it.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.565
-Last time you were here --
thank you for being on --

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:08.000
you Zoomed --
you were Zooming from Australia.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.336
And, now, you're back home,
which is great.

00:01:11.336 --> 00:01:12.805
-It's so nice to be back.

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:15.341
Like, I loved Australia,
but, you know, it's nice.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:16.776
We were there at a great time,

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:19.745
and I think we've come home
at a good time, hopefully.

00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:22.482
-Yeah. You and your husband,
Ben Falcone,

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:25.984
who's very talented, you do this
very fun thing together.

00:01:25.984 --> 00:01:27.753
It's called Beer O'Clock.

00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:29.555
-Sure.
-Yeah.

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:31.891
-I think we all --
Don't we all do Beer O'Clock?

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.893
-I think we all do Beer O'Clock.
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:36.196
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:38.464
But it just kind of
came out of, I guess,

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:40.165
the quarantining,
I guess, right?

00:01:40.165 --> 00:01:43.436
And it's, like, kind of saying
it's the end of the day, or...?

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:45.671
-It was just all --
like, he's so --

00:01:45.671 --> 00:01:47.874
Ben is so wonderfully weird
and, like,

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:50.042
he'll just make videos
sometimes,

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:53.078
and I have no idea he's doing
it, and then it just shows up.

00:01:53.078 --> 00:01:56.749
And he started doing this
weird thing where he was doing

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:59.686
Beer O'Clock, and then, like,
started doing it together.

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:03.322
And we were in -- just to make
it weirder, the last one we did,

00:02:03.322 --> 00:02:05.024
where I believe we're in wigs.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.058
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.058 --> 00:02:07.360
-We do a movie group.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.195
We were dressed up as
two of the guys from "Argo,"

00:02:10.195 --> 00:02:13.999
which is why we're in wigs
and, like, '70s clothes.

00:02:13.999 --> 00:02:16.402
'Cause we dress up as people
from the movies.

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:19.872
And then it was Beer O'Clock,
so we just combined them.

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:22.375
It literally is like,
"How dumb can it be?"

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:24.110
And then he goes,
"We should post this."

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:26.379
Like, it's -- There is no use
to the world.

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:28.381
There's no information given.

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:30.550
But we will dance in our kitchen
with beer.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.653
-And it just made me laugh. It
made millions of people laugh.

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:36.922
I want to show -- here's
Beer O'Clock incorporating wigs.

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:38.858
Take a look at this --
Melissa and Ben.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:42.762 line:0%
♪♪

00:02:42.762 --> 00:02:45.498
-What's in the can?

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:49.601
-Aaah!

00:02:49.601 --> 00:02:53.239
[ Cheers and applause ]
-What?

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:55.575
-It's so stupid.

00:02:55.575 --> 00:02:57.610
It's dumber than I remember.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.844
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.844 --> 00:03:01.814
-How many wigs do you own?

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:04.783
-Ooh. Oh, God.

00:03:04.783 --> 00:03:06.719
It's somewhere in the 60s.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:09.122
Like, I have --
I mean, they're always with me.

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:12.325
There's -- I mean, there's,
like, if you need an Amadeus.

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:13.893
-Oh, that's a good one.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.928
-If you're feeling like
an Orphan Annie.

00:03:15.928 --> 00:03:17.897
-Oh, Annie's a great costume.
-And also,

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:21.667
I've gotten into some of these,
which -- pretty --

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:23.502
pretty terrifying for the kids.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:25.104
-Is that Boris Johnson?

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:28.407
Who is that?
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:30.309
-It is now.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:32.611
It's his actual skin.

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:35.515
Like, just, you know,
they really change you.

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:38.851
A short-cropped man's wig
really bothers Ben.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:39.785
[ Laughter ]
-Oh.

00:03:39.785 --> 00:03:41.620
-I put this one on backwards,

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.323
and it gives me
a little bit of a darker edge,

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:47.726
and it really troubles him.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:47.726 --> 00:03:51.030
-Actually, in real life, you are
as much fun as you'd think.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.800
-[ Laughs ]
-And you are, also, I would say,

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.936
probably one of the best friends
out there.

00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:59.104
And I'm saying this
because I saw something

00:03:59.104 --> 00:04:00.840
recently that was posted.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.676
Mariska Hargitay is a friend of
yours, and she broke her ankle.

00:04:04.676 --> 00:04:07.112
And you could've -- you texted.
You probably did.

00:04:07.112 --> 00:04:09.849
You sent her flowers, whatever
a friend would do and say,

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:10.983
"Hey, get well."

00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:13.219
Instead, you took it
to another level.

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:16.356
Can you tell our audience
what you did?

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:19.692
-Well, yeah, she, like, rolled
her ankle, and it was, like,

00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:22.361
a really --
a really severe break.

00:04:22.361 --> 00:04:24.864
And she's a really good texter,
and I'm terrible.

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:27.299
Sometimes, like, I don't --
like, I put my phone

00:04:27.299 --> 00:04:29.102
in my purse for three days,
and then people are like,

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:30.436
"Where are you?"

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.304
And she sent me
a really funny text of like,

00:04:32.304 --> 00:04:35.307
"Oh, don't worry, I'm just here
in a lot of pain, recovering.

00:04:35.307 --> 00:04:39.545
I guess you're too busy
to respond to my texts."

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:42.148
And so I was like,
"Okay, game on."

00:04:42.148 --> 00:04:44.083
So, now, I have to make it
seem like "this is why

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:47.986
I didn't get back to you, 'cause
I was petitioning for prayers."

00:04:47.986 --> 00:04:51.057
And so we literally jumped
in the car and ran over,

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:53.359
as you do in Burbank,
to Wienerschnitzel.

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:55.827
-Yeah.
-And I just jumped up and down,

00:04:55.827 --> 00:04:59.398
and cars honked, and I think
they were like, "What?"

00:04:59.398 --> 00:05:01.867
-I wanted to show
a little video.

00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:03.136
Here's Melissa McCarthy
out there

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:05.171
supporting her friend
Mariska Hargitay.

00:05:07.439 --> 00:05:10.410
[ Laughs ]
So fun.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.445
[ Horn honks ]
There, you got a honk!

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:14.414
[ Horn honks ]
That's what I'm talking about

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:15.715
You got a couple --
you got a honk.

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:19.118
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:21.521
-I can't tell
if they were thinking like,

00:05:21.521 --> 00:05:23.055
"Get well, Mariska,"
or if they were like,

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:24.690
"Get out of the way!"
-Yeah.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.492
No, they loved it. I loved it.

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:29.227
I want to talk about
"Nine Perfect Strangers."

00:05:29.227 --> 00:05:32.098
This is what you were filming
in Australia, correct?

00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:33.632
-Yeah.

00:05:33.632 --> 00:05:36.502
-It premieres this Wednesday.

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:37.603
-It's really a great --

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:39.571
It was such a great book,
and I loved it.

00:05:39.571 --> 00:05:43.476
And then when Nicole Kidman
calls and is like,

00:05:43.476 --> 00:05:45.043
"Do you want to --
Do you want to do something?"

00:05:45.043 --> 00:05:48.413
I basically was like, "I don't
care what it is," or, you know,

00:05:48.413 --> 00:05:51.451
if I'm on or off screen,
"yes" was my first answer.

00:05:51.451 --> 00:05:53.419
And then it ended up being --
we weren't supposed to do it

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:56.088
in Australia, and it just seems,
like, now, I can't imagine it

00:05:56.088 --> 00:05:57.923
not being done there.

00:05:57.923 --> 00:06:00.659
The scenery is, like,
the whole -- the whole group,

00:06:00.659 --> 00:06:03.729
everybody that came together
to do it was --

00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:06.666
It was so fun and especially
during such weird times,

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:08.234
where we'd all been so isolated,

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:12.071
to suddenly have this little
group of, you know,

00:06:12.071 --> 00:06:15.742
12, 13 actors and a casting crew
kind of bubble together was --

00:06:15.742 --> 00:06:18.878
it was really fun, and it's --
like, I'll never forget it.

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:20.046
-Amazing cast.

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:23.449
Regina Hall, Bobby Cannavale,
Michael Shannon.

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:25.017
-Michael Shannon, yeah.

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:26.852
-Michael Shannon's so funny,

00:06:26.852 --> 00:06:29.421
and he doesn't get to be
that funny in these movies.

00:06:29.421 --> 00:06:31.323
And every time he does,
he shines.

00:06:31.323 --> 00:06:32.891
Watch Michael Shannon. So good.

00:06:32.891 --> 00:06:34.493
-He's so funny.

00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:36.795
Like, I don't -- Yeah, I think
most people don't get that

00:06:36.795 --> 00:06:39.665
he is, like --
as good of an actor as he is --

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:42.701
like, he's such a great actor,
but also he's so weirdly funny.

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:44.270
And it's the same with Bobby.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.371
Like, he always plays,
like, this tough guy.

00:06:46.371 --> 00:06:49.242
Bobby is, like, the weirdest,
funniest guy in the world.

00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:53.980
Regina's really funny, so it's
this weird mix of, you know,

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:57.049
this odd group of kind of
damaged people shows up,

00:06:57.049 --> 00:07:01.186
and, you know, have very strange
tactics for getting better,

00:07:01.186 --> 00:07:03.622
many of which they don't
even know about yet.

00:07:03.622 --> 00:07:06.058
But it was such a blast to do.

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:08.894
Like, even, you know, some of
the scenes are pretty emotional,

00:07:08.894 --> 00:07:11.464
but there was enough time --
I'll just say this.

00:07:11.464 --> 00:07:14.499
Shannon does
some high-pants dancing.

00:07:14.499 --> 00:07:16.535
That means everything.

00:07:16.535 --> 00:07:18.771
-He's coming on.
Let's see if we can get him

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:20.639
to do a little high-pants
dancing.

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:22.240
It would be fantastic.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:26.679
But basically all these people
go to this, like, spa,

00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:28.947
meditation retreat
type of place,

00:07:28.947 --> 00:07:31.918
and you start finding out
each -- more and more details,

00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:34.186
like it or not --
about all of the characters.

00:07:34.186 --> 00:07:35.220
And it's well done.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.356
-Yeah, it's not
always flattering.

00:07:37.356 --> 00:07:40.626
It's like, everybody's kind of
a mess in their own way.

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:42.928
And then they all just start
kind of, like,

00:07:42.928 --> 00:07:48.066
clashing together, and it's fun,
it's wild, it's trippy.

00:07:48.066 --> 00:07:50.168
And also I thought it was
kind of nice that,

00:07:50.168 --> 00:07:52.438
at such a weird time,
that it was, like,

00:07:52.438 --> 00:07:55.407
strangers helping people,
which is -- to me, it was

00:07:55.407 --> 00:07:58.711
a nice, like, reaffirming thing
that in this crazy time,

00:07:58.711 --> 00:08:01.346
I'm like, "Yeah, sometimes,
like, even strangers

00:08:01.346 --> 00:08:02.982
are going to be the ones
to help you."

00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:05.250
-Yeah, I agree.
I want to show a clip.

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:08.154
Here's Melissa McCarthy
in "Nine Perfect Strangers."

00:08:08.154 --> 00:08:09.388
Take a look.

00:08:09.388 --> 00:08:11.190
-Meet my beautiful,
beautiful girls.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.426
Wife Heather, daughter Zoe.

00:08:13.426 --> 00:08:15.161
-Hi.
-Pleasure to meet you.

00:08:15.161 --> 00:08:17.496
-Likewise.
So, what brings you here?

00:08:17.496 --> 00:08:19.398
'Cause I have to say
you look pretty perfect.

00:08:19.398 --> 00:08:22.034
-Why would you say something
like that?

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:24.536
-I meant it as a compliment.

00:08:24.536 --> 00:08:26.305
I'm sorry.

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:27.940
-I think we're gonna explore
the hot springs,

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.575
maybe get a quick soak,

00:08:29.575 --> 00:08:33.078
do a few laps in the pool,
maybe even visit the sacred spa.

00:08:33.078 --> 00:08:34.447
-Should I show you
the hot spring?

00:08:34.447 --> 00:08:35.348
-Please do.

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:37.182
Ciao, Frances.
-Ciao.

00:08:37.182 --> 00:08:40.186
-Come on, ladies.
Jump-start on bliss.

00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:41.954
-Nice to meet you.

00:08:45.758 --> 00:08:47.092
Heather's a bitch.

00:08:47.092 --> 00:08:49.328
-There you go.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:51.196
That's how you lay it in there.
Come on.

00:08:51.196 --> 00:08:53.432
Melissa McCarthy, everyone!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:53.432 --> 00:08:58.738
"Nine Perfect Strangers,"
premieres Wednesday on Hulu.

